---
registryId: dynamodb
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Amazon DynamoDB?

**Amazon DynamoDB is a serverless, fully managed, distributed NoSQL database that
stores key-value and document data.** Applications read and mutate items through
primary keys, secondary indexes, and bounded APIs instead of sending arbitrary joins
to a relational query planner.

DynamoDB matters when a request path needs predictable single-digit millisecond
performance while traffic and data grow. AWS owns servers, partition management,
replication inside a Region, maintenance, and service scaling. Your team still owns
the data model, key distribution, consistency contract, capacity and quota boundaries,
retry behavior, and recovery drills.

The core invariant is: **every important access pattern must map to a key-based
operation whose traffic remains distributed.** A table with ample aggregate capacity
can still throttle when one partition-key value or GSI key range receives too much
work.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Strong fit" eyebrow="Known access paths" icon="key" tone="blue" %}
Use DynamoDB for high-scale request paths with known point reads, bounded range
queries, conditional writes, and simple item-centered transactions.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Weak fit" eyebrow="Ad hoc relationships" icon="warning" tone="rose" %}
Do not expect joins, arbitrary multi-attribute filtering, or analytical scans to remain
efficient as a transactional table grows.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Design responsibility" eyebrow="Serverless is not schemaless thinking" icon="shield" tone="green" %}
AWS operates the service. The application must encode entities and relationships into
keys, test skew, make retries safe, and prove restore procedures.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Build the mental model from tables, items, and keys

A **table** is a collection of **items**. An item is a collection of named
**attributes**, similar to a row with scalar, set, list, map, or document values. Each
item can have different non-key attributes, but every item must satisfy the table's
primary-key schema. The complete item, including attribute names, must remain within
the 400 KB item limit.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Partition key" eyebrow="Placement and lookup" icon="hash" tone="blue" %}
DynamoDB hashes this value to place an item. A simple primary key contains only this
attribute, so its value must be unique per item.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Sort key" eyebrow="Ordered item collection" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
With a composite primary key, items may share a partition key and are ordered by sort
key. Prefixes and ranges can retrieve related items without a scan.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Primary key" eyebrow="Unique identity" icon="key" tone="green" %}
The partition key alone, or the partition-key and sort-key pair, uniquely identifies
one item and defines the direct access contract.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

For a commerce table, `PK = CUSTOMER#c-42` and
`SK = ORDER#2026-07-24T09:30:00Z#o-2048` can group one customer's orders and sort them
by time. A separate order item can use `PK = ORDER#o-2048` for a direct lookup.
Duplicating selected facts is often intentional when it serves two bounded access
patterns without a join.

Read the official
[core-components guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.CoreComponents.html)
when the distinction between item identity, item collections, and secondary indexes
needs a deeper walkthrough.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Design from access patterns before drawing a table

Relational modeling often starts with normalized entities and derives queries later.
DynamoDB modeling reverses that sequence: list the application's reads and writes,
then choose keys and indexes that answer them directly.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Name each operation" label="Contract" %}
Write concrete requests such as “get order by ID,” “list a customer's newest orders,”
or “transition one order from pending to paid.”
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Define bounds" label="Shape" %}
Record result count, item size, read freshness, write atomicity, peak rate, tenant
isolation, and retention for every operation.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Assign keys and indexes" label="Model" %}
Map direct identity to the base key, ordered relationships to sort-key ranges, and
required alternate lookup dimensions to a secondary index.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Test skew and failure" label="Prove" %}
Load-test realistic key distributions, retries, concurrency races, GSI pressure, and
restore procedures before treating the schema as production-ready.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Access-pattern review

- Prefer `GetItem` when the complete primary key is known.
- Prefer `Query` when one partition-key value and an optional sort-key condition bound
  the result.
- Treat `Scan` as deliberate table-wide work, not the fallback for an unmodeled
  request path.
- Remember that a `FilterExpression` is applied after DynamoDB reads candidate items;
  it does not turn a broad read into a cheap key lookup.
- Design both the base table and every GSI for high-cardinality, well-distributed
  partition-key values.

AWS's current
[DynamoDB data-modeling guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/data-modeling.html)
describes single-table and multiple-table foundations. Single-table design is a
technique, not a requirement: use it when colocated item collections simplify real
operations, not as a badge of sophistication.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Separate aggregate capacity from hot-key pressure

DynamoDB rounds write capacity by item size: one write capacity unit supports one
write per second for an item up to 1 KB. A strongly consistent read of an item up to
4 KB consumes one read capacity unit; an eventually consistent read of the same size
uses half as much read capacity.

Each partition is designed for up to 1,000 write capacity units and 3,000 read
capacity units per second. Adaptive capacity helps uneven workloads, but it does not
remove the per-partition ceiling. The same distribution problem applies independently
to the base table and every GSI.

{% interactive-block id="technology/dynamodb-key-distribution-capacity-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/dynamodb/data/key-distribution-capacity-model.json" /%}

### Choose capacity mode from traffic evidence

- **On-demand:** pays per request and removes a configured table-level capacity
  target. It is a strong default for new, spiky, or unpredictable workloads, but hot
  key ranges, configured maximum throughput, account quotas, and rapid growth beyond
  warm throughput still matter.
- **Provisioned:** configures RCU and WCU directly, optionally with auto scaling. It
  fits steady, measurable workloads when the team can manage headroom and scale before
  scheduled bursts.
- **Both modes:** require realistic partition-key distribution, per-index monitoring,
  bounded clients, and diagnosis from the throttling reason rather than a generic
  “add capacity” response.

Use the official
[partition-key guidance](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-design.html)
and
[capacity-mode guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/CostOptimization_TableCapacityMode.html)
to verify service behavior as limits and features evolve.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Add secondary indexes only for named queries

A secondary index stores a separate, projected view of table items under another key
schema. It makes an alternate query efficient, but it adds write work, storage, and a
new distribution surface.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Base table" eyebrow="Authoritative key" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Supports eventually consistent reads by default and optional strongly consistent reads
for `GetItem`, `Query`, and `Scan`.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Local secondary index" eyebrow="Same partition key" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
Uses the base partition key with another sort key. It shares table capacity and can
support strong reads, but must be created with the table and has item-collection
constraints.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Global secondary index" eyebrow="Alternate partition key" icon="network" tone="green" %}
Can use different partition and sort keys. Updates are asynchronous, reads are always
eventually consistent, and its partitions and capacity are independent of the base
table.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

In provisioned mode, a GSI has separate RCU and WCU. If an index cannot absorb base
table updates, DynamoDB can throttle the base-table write to maintain index
consistency. A low-cardinality GSI partition key such as `status = OPEN` can therefore
become hot even when the base table's customer IDs distribute perfectly.

Review AWS's
[read-consistency contract](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.ReadConsistency.html)
and
[GSI back-pressure guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/gsi-throttling.html)
before promising read-after-write behavior or diagnosing write throttling.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Choose consistency, correctness, and recovery together

Read consistency answers what a read may observe. Conditional writes and transactions
answer whether concurrent mutations preserve business invariants. Backups answer how
operators recover from a valid API call that produced the wrong business state. None
of these controls substitutes for the other two.

{% interactive-block id="technology/dynamodb-consistency-index-recovery-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/dynamodb/data/consistency-index-recovery-model.json" /%}

### Correctness boundaries

- A single `PutItem`, `UpdateItem`, or `DeleteItem` is atomic for its item.
- A condition expression rejects a write when the current item no longer satisfies a
  version, existence, state, or numeric invariant.
- `TransactWriteItems` groups multiple item actions into one ACID operation that
  commits or cancels as a unit.
- A strongly consistent pre-read does not lock the item. Use a condition or
  transaction at mutation time to prevent lost updates and invalid transitions.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Make throttles, retries, and stream delivery harmless

Transient throttling is part of the API contract. A reliable client slows down,
retries only safe work, and preserves the identity of the business operation.

{% system-flow title="Bounded DynamoDB mutation and event path" caption="The write invariant is enforced before the table changes. Stream-triggered consumers still need destination-side idempotency because failed batches can be retried." %}
{% system-node title="Application command" eyebrow="Identify" icon="users" tone="blue" %}
Carry a stable order, request, or event identifier so a retry refers to the same
business operation.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Condition or transaction" eyebrow="Protect" icon="shield" tone="violet" %}
Check the current invariant atomically and commit one item or an all-or-nothing item
set.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="DynamoDB table" eyebrow="Persist" icon="database" tone="green" %}
Return success only after the write is durably persisted, or return a specific
condition, transaction, quota, or throttling failure.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Stream consumer" eyebrow="React" icon="activity" tone="cyan" %}
Process change records with an idempotency boundary so a retried Lambda batch does not
repeat a charge, email, or projection mutation.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Retry rules

- Use AWS SDK retry behavior or bounded exponential backoff with jitter for throttling
  and retryable service failures.
- Inspect `ThrottlingReason` and the affected resource ARN; table capacity, key-range
  pressure, and GSI pressure require different fixes.
- `BatchWriteItem` is not atomic as a whole. Retry only returned
  `UnprocessedItems`, not every request that already succeeded.
- Use conditional writes, transaction client tokens where supported, and stable
  operation IDs to make repeated requests safe.
- Send exhausted work to a visible failure path with enough context to replay it
  deliberately; do not retry forever inside a request thread.

DynamoDB Streams captures item changes for event-driven processing. A Lambda trigger
retries a failed batch, so the consumer must be idempotent even when the source record
itself has a stable event ID.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Treat backups as a recovery workflow, not a checkbox

Point-in-time recovery continuously captures table recovery points at per-second
granularity for a configurable period from 1 to 35 days. A restore creates a **new
table**; it does not rewind the current table in place. The team must validate the
restored data and deliberately reapply or verify settings such as IAM policies, auto
scaling, alarms, streams, TTL, tags, deletion protection, and PITR itself.

On-demand backups are useful for named milestones and longer-lived snapshots.
Replicas, global tables, GSIs, and Streams improve availability or propagate changes,
but they can also propagate an accidental delete or corrupt write. They are not
independent rollback history.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Detect and contain" label="Stop" %}
Block the bad writer, capture the failure window, and preserve logs before another
deployment or repair changes the evidence.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Restore to a new table" label="Recover" %}
Choose a point before the mutation and restore without consuming source-table
provisioned throughput.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Validate data and settings" label="Prove" %}
Check representative keys, counts, indexes, encryption, access policy, streams, TTL,
alarms, and application compatibility.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Cut over and reconcile" label="Resume" %}
Redirect traffic deliberately, reconcile valid writes after the restore point, monitor
the new table, and keep a rollback path until the incident is closed.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

See the official
[PITR guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Point-in-time-recovery.html)
and
[restore behavior](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/pointintimerecovery_restores.html)
when writing the runbook.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Put the contracts into focused code

The first example turns named access patterns into concrete primary-key, range-query,
and transaction requests. The key strings make entity type and relationship intent
visible during debugging.

